==German==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|gmh|de}} {{m|gmh|ziehen}}, from {{etyl|goh|de}} {{m|goh|ziohan}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|de}} {{m|gem-pro|*teuhaną}}. Cognate with {{etyl|nds|-}} {{m|nds|tehn}}, {{etyl|got|-}} {{m|got|𐍄𐌹𐌿𐌷𐌰𐌽}}, {{etyl|dum|-}} {{m|dum|tīen}}, {{etyl|ang|-}} {{m|ang|tēon}}, {{etyl|non|-}} {{m|non|toga}}, {{etyl|fy|-}} {{m|fy|tsjen}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|De-ziehen.ogg|Audio|lang=de}}
* {{IPA|/ˈtsiːən/|/ˈtsiːn/|lang=de}}

===Verb===
{{de-verb-strong|class=2|zieht|zog|gezogen|past subjunctive=zöge|auxiliary=both}}

# {{context|transitive|or|intransitive|auxiliary: “haben”|lang=de}} to [[pull]] (e.g., a door handle); to [[drag]]
# {{context|transitive|auxiliary: “haben”|lang=de}} to [[draw]] (e.g. a weapon); to [[extract]]; to [[puff]]
# {{context|transitive|auxiliary: “haben”|lang=de}} to [[draw]] (a conclusion, lesson, etc.)
#* '''2010''', ''{{w|Der Spiegel}}'', issue [http://www.spiegel.de/spiegel/print/index-2010-25.html 25/2010], page 77:
#*:Es gilt deshalb, die richtigen Lehren aus der Krise zu '''ziehen''', aus den Fehlern der Vergangenheit zu lernen, um die Zukunft zu sichern.
#*::Therefore it is necessary to draw the right lessons from the crisis, to learn from the mistakes of the past for securing the future.
# {{context|impersonal|intransitive|lang=de}} to [[be]] [[drafty]]
#: ''Es '''zieht'''.'' — “It’s drafty.”
# {{context|intransitive|auxiliary: “sein”|lang=de}} to [[move]]; to [[migrate]]
#: ''Ich '''ziehe''' nach Hamburg, aber mein Bruder '''zieht''' in eine andere Stadt.''
# {{context|intransitive|auxiliary: “sein”|lang=de}} to [[roam]]
# {{context|reflexive|auxiliary: “haben”|lang=de}} to [[stretch]]; to [[warp]]

